    "created_at": "2015-09-23T14:32:06Z",
    "updated_at": "2015-09-23T14:32:06Z",
    "body": "> > by choosing not to expose setItem as an own property.\n> \n> You mean, by following the spec.\n\nhttps://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/webstorage.html#storage-2\n\n> The supported property names on a Storage object are the keys of each key/value pair currently present in the list associated with the object, in the order that the keys were last added to the storage area.\n\n``` js\n// Firefox 40\nlocalStorage.hasOwnProperty( 'foo' ); // false\nlocalStorage.setItem( 'foo', 123 );\nlocalStorage.hasOwnProperty( 'foo' ); // true\nlocalStorage.foo; // \"123\"\n\nlocalStorage.hasOwnProperty( 'setItem' ); // false\nlocalStorage.setItem( 'setItem', 123 );\nlocalStorage.hasOwnProperty( 'setItem' ); // false\nlocalStorage.setItem; // [native code]\n```\n\n@Ms2ger I'm not sure I understand how that is following the spec. I don't see any reference in the above, or other meta references, that explain why a browser should skip this algorithm if the name exists in the inheritance chain (e.g. as an interface method). While I'm not advocating for this behaviour, seeing that there is no `OverrideBuiltins` in the spec, I would expect Firefox to have the own property shadow the method.\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
